WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.560
A mouse taste cell can detect sweet, bitter or umami.

2
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.880
But this cell has no conventional vesicular synapse.

3
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.080
So how does the news reach the nerve fibre?

4
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.360
Everything in this film is a mouse result.

5
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.320
None of it is a human map.

6
00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.870
These are type II taste cells.

7
00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:28.950
They carry the receptors for sweet, bitter and umami.

8
00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:32.130
Those are G protein coupled receptors.

9
00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:37.170
When the cell is depolarized, it releases ATP through a channel.

10
00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:41.270
The nerve fibre beside it carries an ATP receptor.

11
00:00:42.183 --> 00:00:50.123
Taruno and colleagues found that CALHM1 is required for taste-evoked ATP release

12
00:00:50.123 --> 00:00:52.003
from mouse type II cells.

13
00:00:52.723 --> 00:00:58.743
They used knockout mice, isolated cells, taste tissue, nerve recordings and

14
00:00:58.743 --> 00:01:02.643
behaviour. Without that channel, the release fails.

15
00:01:04.183 --> 00:01:08.163
Sour and salty recognition was mostly normal in the assays they tested.

16
00:01:08.963 --> 00:01:11.683
That fits release by a different route in those cells.

17
00:01:12.743 --> 00:01:17.023
That does not mean that sodium signalling never uses CALHM channels.

18
00:01:17.933 --> 00:01:19.293
A puzzle remained.

19
00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:23.333
CALHM1 alone is slow.

20
00:01:24.413 --> 00:01:30.493
In mouse N2a cells, recorded by whole-cell voltage clamp, it activated with a time

21
00:01:30.493 --> 00:01:32.753
constant above 500 milliseconds.

22
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:39.373
Voltage clamp holds the membrane potential fixed while the channel currents are recorded.

23
00:01:39.617 --> 00:01:44.437
Ma and colleagues deleted CALHM3 in mice.

24
00:01:45.677 --> 00:01:48.837
Taste-evoked ATP release from taste buds was abolished.

25
00:01:49.737 --> 00:01:56.937
In the cultured cells, CALHM1 with CALHM3 activated in about 10

26
00:01:56.937 --> 00:02:00.617
milliseconds. That is a speed-up of more than 50-fold.

27
00:02:01.377 --> 00:02:07.277
The authors concluded that CALHM3 supplies the rapid gating that a

28
00:02:07.277 --> 00:02:09.577
CALHM1-only account lacked.

29
00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:12.230
Keep the limits in mind.

30
00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:16.130
The exact times depend on voltage and protocol.

31
00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:21.730
They are properties of cultured cells, not delays measured in a living animal.

32
00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:24.370
So here is the rule to keep.

33
00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:34.310
In mouse type II taste cells, ATP leaves through CALHM channels, and CALHM3 speeds the gating.
